\import Arith.Nat
\import Data.Bool
\import Data.Maybe
\import Data.String
\import Semantics.LambdaBoxEval
-- `wrap63` is SHARED with the backend (`Compiler/Int63.ard`), not re-derived here: that
-- the two sides normalize with the same function is the whole content of the
-- int63 representation.
\import Compiler.Int63 (wrap63, wrap64u)
\import Compiler.LambdaBox
\import Set
\import Compiler.StringUtil (intEqB)

-- =============================================================================
-- One instance of `LBSpec` (Semantics/LambdaBoxEval.ard): what λ□'s axioms MEAN.
--
-- The mirror image of `Compiler/JavaAxioms.ard`, which says what they COMPILE TO. Same
-- table, read the other way: `javaAxioms` maps `c__Nat__add` to the runtime
-- constant `Rt.PRIM_ADD_LONG`, and this module says that `Nat.add` denotes
-- addition. Neither is derivable from the other -- that is the whole point of
-- having both, because an agreement between them is a real check, and their
-- DISAGREEMENTS are findings (see `int63` below).
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE SOURCE LANGUAGE'S ARITHMETIC, NOT THE JVM'S
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The temptation here is to define these the way `Semantics/RtLong.ard` does, on
-- 64-bit wrapping arithmetic, which would make every agreement check pass. That
-- would be worthless: it bakes the backend's behaviour into the specification
-- the backend is checked against, and the one mismatch this project has
-- documented from the start would become invisible.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- CORRECTION, and it retracts limitation (L).
--
-- This module used to define the `Nat.*` family on UNBOUNDED naturals, on the
-- grounds that "Lean's `Nat` is arbitrary-precision". That is true of LEAN and
-- false of the λ□ that lean-to-lambdabox emits, which is what we compile. Its
-- erasure (`LeanToLambdaBox/Erasure.lean`, `visitLiteral`) turns a `Nat`
-- literal into `prim ⟨.primInt, n⟩` in `.machine` mode -- bounded by
-- `BitVec.intMax 63`, and it PANICS rather than exceeding it -- with
-- `PrimModel .primInt := BitVec 63` (`Basic.lean`). Its own summary says it:
-- "Turn Nat literals into lambdabox primitive i63 (panic on overflow) ... the
-- usual functions on `Nat` are treated as axioms".
--
-- So there is no unbounded integer anywhere in the λ□ we receive. THERE IS ONE
-- INTEGER KIND, i63, and both families range over it. What made the old reading
-- look defensible is that it is a true statement about the source language one
-- step further back.
--
-- The consequences of the correction:
--   * `natRun` wraps `add` and `mul` at 2^63 (they are the only operations that
--     can leave the range), so it now agrees with `Rt.PRIM_*_INT63`.
--   * `Compiler/JavaAxioms.ard` routes `Nat.add`/`Nat.mul` to that family accordingly.
--   * limitation (L) in `Proof/Statement.ard` is WITHDRAWN, and with it the
--     `Nat`-overflow refutation that rested on it. That refutation was a proof
--     that this module disagreed with the backend, which was true -- and this
--     module was the side that was wrong.
--
-- WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE is `Nat.sub`. Lean's subtraction truncates at zero,
-- which is Lean's convention and not i63's, so it keeps its own runtime
-- constant; `Rt.java` records that sharing the wrapping one produced a real
-- wrong answer (`const_fold` returned 2048 instead of 4772). "One integer kind"
-- is a statement about the VALUES, not about the operations.
--
--   * `prim_*_int` -- λ□'s primitive int is MetaRocq's `EPrimitive.v`
--     `primIntModel (i : PrimInt63.int)`, i.e. `Uint63`: a 63-bit integer with
--     cyclic arithmetic (mod 2^63), matching lean-to-lambdabox's
--     `PrimModel .primInt := BitVec 63` and the Rocq refman's `Uint63`. Hence
--     `wrap63`, which the BACKEND shares: with `primRepr := int63Repr` the
--     generated code runs `Rt.PRIM_*_INT63`, normalized by that same function.
--     So for this family the int63/int64 gap is CLOSED rather than merely
--     documented -- it survives only under `longRepr`/`rtLong`.
--
-- WHAT IS SHARED AND WHAT IS NOT, and the difference is the whole design:
--   * `wrap63` comes from `Compiler/Int63.ard` and is shared with the BACKEND, which
--     normalizes its literals and its `Rt.PRIM_*_INT63` family with the very
--     same function (`Semantics/RtInt63.ard` models it). Three copies that
--     happened to agree would state the correspondence far more weakly than
--     one definition used by both sides.
--   * `wrap64` (`Semantics/RtLong.ard`) is NOT shared with it, though the two have
--     the same shape. That one is the JVM's rule (JLS 4.2.1/4.2.2), a different
--     specification which λ□ never asked for, and collapsing them would hide
--     exactly the difference `oracleDisagrees` exists to expose.
--   * `intEqB` is shared with everything, carrying no specification at all (see
--     its comment in `Compiler/StringUtil.ard`).
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- NAMED GAPS, all deliberate and all `nothing`, hence `rStuck`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--   * `Array.*` -- Lean's arrays are mutable and `@[extern]`; modelling them
--     needs a store on this side, which λ□'s value domain (terms) has no room
--     for. `Semantics/RtLong.ard` omits them too.
--   * `Nat.decEq`/`decLe`/`decLt`, `Eq.rec`/`Eq.ndrec` -- these return
--     `Decidable`, whose constructors carry erased PROOFS, so the value depends
--     on an inductive declaration this module would have to invent.
--   * `Int.*` -- Lean's machine `Int` erases to the same λ□ primitive int as
--     `Nat`, so these are expressible, but each needs its own convention
--     (`ediv`/`emod` are Euclidean). 8 occurrences in the corpus, all in
--     `leanbench-deriv`.
--   * `Nat.pow` -- expressible now that the family is i63 (the result wraps
--     like `mul`), but left out until a program needs it. 2 occurrences.
-- Each is a name `javaAxioms` DOES realize, so each is a place the backend
-- compiles something this module cannot explain. The counts are the census in
-- `ModelChecks/Agreement.ard`'s header, which also says which way each gap cuts --
-- all of these are gaps in `Semantics/RtLong.ard` too. (`Nat.div`/`mod`/`ble`/
-- `blt` used to be covered here and not there; `Semantics/RtLong.ard` now models
-- their `PRIM_*_LONG` constants, and `Proof/OracleShipped.ard` proves the two
-- sides agree on every name.)
-- =============================================================================

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The values this oracle produces and consumes.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- λ□ has no boolean primitive: Lean's `Bool` is an ordinary two-constructor
-- inductive, and `Bool.false`/`Bool.true` are constructors 0 and 1 of it (see
-- any `Imported/Leanbench*.ard`: `ConstructorBody "Bool.false" 0` first). The
-- tags therefore agree with `Semantics/RtLong.ard`'s `mkBool`, which is why
-- `agrees` can compare the two without knowing about either.
--
-- THE INDUCTIVE ID IS PART OF THE VALUE, and it has to be the program's own:
-- `evalIota` checks that a scrutinee's constructor belongs to the inductive the
-- `case` names, so a boolean built with the wrong id would compute and then be
-- stuck at the `case` that reads it. This is the Lean frontend's root-module
-- `Bool`; a program with a different one needs a different instance of `LBSpec`,
-- which is what "the oracle is an assumption" means concretely.
\func boolInd : InductiveId => \new InductiveId (\new Kername (mpFile nil) "Bool") 0

\func lbBool (b : Bool) : LBTerm =>
  construct boolInd (\case b \with { | true => 1 | false => 0 }) nil

-- A primitive int, as both semantics' arguments and results are spelled.
--
-- NORMALIZATION IS NO LONGER A DISCIPLINE. `PrimModel primInt63` is
-- `Fin int63Mod`, so building a payload goes through `wrap63` by construction
-- and there is no way to produce an out-of-range λ□ integer -- the `norm63`
-- calls the operations below used to make by hand are now the type's job. That
-- is the difference between "every clause remembers to wrap" and "an
-- un-wrapped value cannot be written down".
\func lbInt (i : Int) : LBTerm => prim (primInt63, wrap63 i)

-- One primitive of each kind, decoded; `nothing` on anything else.
\func int63Of (t : LBTerm) : Maybe Int \elim t
  | prim (primInt63, x) => just x
  | _ => nothing

\func int64Of (t : LBTerm) : Maybe Int \elim t
  | prim (primInt64, x) => just x
  | _ => nothing

\func bothJust (m1 m2 : Maybe Int) : Maybe (\Sigma Int Int) \elim m1, m2
  | just x, just y => just (x, y)
  | just _, nothing => nothing
  | nothing, _ => nothing

-- Exactly two arguments, each decoded by `f`.
--
-- Split into these small functions, rather than one nested pattern, so that a
-- proof inverting it (`Proof/OracleShipped.ard`) meets one constructor split
-- at a time: the array's shape here, the term's kind in `int63Of`.
\func twoOf (f : LBTerm -> Maybe Int) (args : Array LBTerm) : Maybe (\Sigma Int Int) \elim args
  | x :: y :: nil => bothJust (f x) (f y)
  | _ => nothing

-- The two arguments every axiom here takes, decoded. `nothing` covers both "not
-- two arguments" and "not primitive ints", which are the same kind of failure:
-- the runtime was handed something it does not compute on.
\func twoInts (args : Array LBTerm) : Maybe (\Sigma Int Int) => twoOf int63Of args

-- The same for the other kind. Two extractors rather than one that accepts
-- either: an i64 operation handed i63 operands is a MALFORMED program, not a
-- conversion opportunity, and it should be stuck rather than silently
-- reinterpreted. That is the whole reason the kind is in the term.
\func twoInts64 (args : Array LBTerm) : Maybe (\Sigma Int Int) => twoOf int64Of args

-- A 64-bit primitive, as a term. `wrap64u` normalizes into [0, 2^64), so as
-- with `lbInt` the payload cannot be out of range.
\func lbInt64 (i : Int) : LBTerm => prim (primInt64, wrap64u i)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The table.
--
-- Keyed by `Kername`, as `javaAxioms` is too; the backend compares the two
-- through `Compiler/Mangle.ard`'s injective `mangleKername`, which turns
-- `Kername (mpDot (mpFile nil) "Nat") "add"` into `c_oNat_nadd`. Keeping the
-- structured name here rather than its mangling is what makes this module
-- independent of the backend's naming.
--
-- A walk over a list of names with `kernameEq` rather than `lookupKey`,
-- because `lookupKey` wants a `DecSet` and `Kername` is a record of a
-- `ModPath` and an `Ident`. It used to be a sixteen-deep `\case` chain, which
-- said the same thing but could not be reasoned about uniformly: the list form
-- is what lets `Proof/OracleShipped.ard` prove "every name with an arity
-- here is realized by the backend" by one induction.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\func natKn (name : String) : Kername => \new Kername (mpDot (mpFile nil) "Nat") name

\func rootKn (name : String) : Kername => \new Kername (mpFile nil) name

-- The axioms this oracle computes, in the order the table used to test them.
\func lbAxiomNames : Array Kername =>
  natKn "add" :: natKn "mul" :: natKn "sub" :: natKn "div" ::
  natKn "mod" :: natKn "beq" :: natKn "ble" :: natKn "blt" ::
  rootKn "prim_add_int" :: rootKn "prim_sub_int" ::
  rootKn "prim_mul_int" :: rootKn "prim_eqb_int" ::
  rootKn "prim_add_int64" :: rootKn "prim_sub_int64" ::
  rootKn "prim_mul_int64" :: rootKn "prim_eqb_int64" :: nil

-- One step of the walk. The test is a PARAMETER, so that a proof holding
-- `kernameEq kn k = b` can rewrite it (`StringUtil.lookupStep`'s device).
\func arityStep (hit : Bool) (rest : Maybe Nat) : Maybe Nat \elim hit
  | true => just 2
  | false => rest

\func arityIn (kn : Kername) (ks : Array Kername) : Maybe Nat \elim ks
  | nil => nothing
  | k :: ks => arityStep (kernameEq kn k) (arityIn kn ks)

-- Every axiom below is binary, as `javaAxioms` realizes them: `Rt.java`'s
-- `PRIM_*` and `NAT_*` constants all collect two arguments before computing.
-- Writing the arity down is the same contract `RtSpec.builtinArity` states, and
-- it is what `axiomSat` tests against.
\func axiomArityLb (kn : Kername) : Maybe Nat => arityIn kn lbAxiomNames

-- `0 <= d` for an `Int`: after the `neg 0 = pos 0` identification the
-- negatives are exactly `neg (suc _)`.
\func nonNegLb (d : Int) : Bool
  | pos _ => true
  | neg (suc _) => false

-- `x <= y`, without peeling. `Nat.-` on two `Nat`s is truncating subtraction
-- landing in `Int`, so the sign of `y - x` decides it.
\func natLeB (x y : Nat) : Bool => nonNegLb (y Nat.- x)

-- Lean's truncated subtraction, from the `Int` difference: negatives are 0.
\func truncLb (d : Int) : Int
  | pos k => pos k
  | neg (suc _) => 0

-- Lean's `Nat.div` and `Nat.mod`, BY ZERO INCLUDED: `Nat.div_zero : n / 0 = 0`
-- and `Nat.mod_zero : n % 0 = n` (Lean core, `Nat.div`/`Nat.modCore` are
-- defined with an `if 0 < y` guard whose else-branch is `0` resp. `x`).
--
-- The guard is explicit because Arend's builtins do NOT share the first
-- convention: `Nat.divMod n 0` is `(n, n)`, so `n Nat.div 0` is `n`, checked by
-- `idp`. Only the `mod` half happens to agree. One constructor deep on the
-- divisor, so nothing is peeled.
\func leanDiv (x y : Nat) : Nat \elim y
  | 0 => 0
  | suc y => x Nat.div suc y

\func leanMod (x y : Nat) : Nat \elim y
  | 0 => x
  | suc y => x Nat.mod suc y

-- Lean's `Nat` operations, ON i63 VALUES -- see the header's correction of (L).
--
-- `add` and `mul` WRAP at 2^63, because the values they compute on are i63 and
-- the reference realization of these axioms is CertiRocq's `prim_int63_*`.
-- They are the only two that can leave the range: `sub` truncates downwards,
-- `div`/`mod` shrink, and the comparisons return a `Bool`.
--
-- `sub` TRUNCATES AT ZERO, and that is Lean's convention rather than i63's.
-- This is the one place the two families genuinely differ, which is why
-- `javaAxioms` sends it to `NAT_SUB_*` and not to a wrapping `PRIM_SUB_*`;
-- `Rt.java` records that sharing the wrapping one produced a real wrong answer
-- (`const_fold` returned 2048 instead of 4772). Arend's `Nat.-` truncates into
-- `Int`, so the negative case is clamped here, and no wrap is needed: both
-- arguments are in [0, 2^63) and so is the result.
--
-- CORRECTION: this comment used to say that "`div`/`mod` by zero are zero in
-- Lean ... and Arend's `Nat.div`/`Nat.mod` agree", and used them unguarded.
-- Both halves were wrong. Lean's `n % 0` is `n`, not zero (`Nat.mod_zero`);
-- and Arend's `n Nat.div 0` is `n`, not zero. So `div` by zero computed `x`
-- where Lean -- and `Rt.PRIM_DIV_LONG` -- give `0`, a wrong answer on this side;
-- `mod` was right only because the two mistakes cancelled. `leanDiv`/`leanMod`
-- state Lean's conventions explicitly. Their results cannot leave the range.
\func natRun (op : String) (x y : Nat) : Maybe LBTerm =>
  \case op == "add" \with { | true => just (lbInt (pos (x Nat.+ y))) | false =>
  \case op == "mul" \with { | true => just (lbInt (pos (x Nat.* y))) | false =>
  \case op == "sub" \with { | true => just (lbInt (truncLb (x Nat.- y))) | false =>
  \case op == "div" \with { | true => just (lbInt (pos (leanDiv x y))) | false =>
  \case op == "mod" \with { | true => just (lbInt (pos (leanMod x y))) | false =>
  \case op == "beq" \with { | true => just (lbBool (intEqB (pos x) (pos y))) | false =>
  \case op == "ble" \with { | true => just (lbBool (natLeB x y)) | false =>
  \case op == "blt" \with { | true => just (lbBool (natLeB (suc x) y)) | false =>
    nothing } } } } } } } }

-- The int63 family: cyclic mod 2^63, per `primIntModel`/`Uint63`.
--
-- Each operation normalizes its RESULT, and `prim_eqb_int` normalizes its
-- arguments before comparing -- the same invariant discipline `builtinRunLong`
-- keeps for `PRIM_EQB_LONG`, and for the same reason: a caller handing in an
-- un-normalized literal must not change the answer.
\func int63Run (op : String) (x y : Int) : Maybe LBTerm =>
  \let | a => wrap63 x
       | b => wrap63 y
  \in
  \case op == "prim_add_int" \with {
    | true => just (lbInt (pos (a Nat.+ b))) | false =>
  \case op == "prim_mul_int" \with {
    | true => just (lbInt (pos (a Nat.* b))) | false =>
  -- Subtraction is where UNSIGNED wrapping is visible: `1 - 2` is `2^63 - 1`,
  -- not `-1`. `Nat.-` lands in `Int`, which `wrap63` takes directly.
  \case op == "prim_sub_int" \with {
    | true => just (lbInt (a Nat.- b)) | false =>
  \case op == "prim_eqb_int" \with {
    | true => just (lbBool (intEqB (pos a) (pos b))) | false =>
    nothing } } } }

-- The i64 family: two's-complement 64-bit, i.e. exactly what a JVM `long`
-- does. Operands and results are BIT PATTERNS in [0, 2^64); `wrap64u`
-- normalizes, and `lbInt64` applies it so nothing here can produce an
-- out-of-range payload.
--
-- These names are not in any imported program. They exist because the kind of
-- a value cannot reach the operation that consumes it (a `const` is compiled
-- before its operands), so choosing 64-bit arithmetic means choosing different
-- OPERATIONS as well as different values -- which is what `Compiler/Int64Rewrite.ard`
-- does, and what makes the choice something a semantics can talk about.
\func int64Run (op : String) (x y : Int) : Maybe LBTerm =>
  \let | a => wrap64u x
       | b => wrap64u y
  \in
  \case op == "prim_add_int64" \with {
    | true => just (lbInt64 (pos (a Nat.+ b))) | false =>
  \case op == "prim_mul_int64" \with {
    | true => just (lbInt64 (pos (a Nat.* b))) | false =>
  \case op == "prim_sub_int64" \with {
    | true => just (lbInt64 (a Nat.- b)) | false =>
  -- Bit patterns are already normalized, so this is plain equality -- the same
  -- comparison `Rt.PRIM_EQB_LONG` performs on two `long`s.
  \case op == "prim_eqb_int64" \with {
    | true => just (lbBool (intEqB (pos a) (pos b))) | false =>
    nothing } } } }

-- Dispatch: a `Nat.*` name goes to `natRun` on the two arguments read as
-- naturals, a `prim_*_int` name to `int63Run`. Both require their arguments to
-- be primitive ints ALREADY EVALUATED, which `evalAxiomApp` guarantees: it tests
-- the spine only after both parts of the application have values.
--
-- Anything else is `nothing`, i.e. `rStuck` -- including a `Nat.*` operation
-- handed a non-integer, which is the twin of `builtinRunLong`'s `rStuck` on
-- non-`vLong` arguments.
--
-- The decoded arguments are handed to `natAt`/`rootAt` as PARAMETERS rather
-- than scrutinized by a `\case` here, for the usual reason: a proof can then
-- rewrite them.
\func axiomRunLb (kn : Kername) (args : Array LBTerm) : Maybe LBTerm =>
  \case modPathEq kn.kerModPath (mpDot (mpFile nil) "Nat") \with {
    -- Lean's machine operations, on i63 values with Lean's conventions.
    | true => natAt kn.kerName (twoInts args)
    | false => \case modPathEq kn.kerModPath (mpFile nil) \with {
      -- The root module holds both primitive families, told apart by NAME --
      -- and each accepts only its own kind of operand. An i64 operation handed
      -- i63 values is stuck, not converted: the term would be malformed, and
      -- silently reinterpreting it is exactly what having the kind in the term
      -- is meant to prevent.
      | true => rootAt kn.kerName (twoInts args) (twoInts64 args)
      | false => nothing
    }
  }

\func natAt (op : String) (m : Maybe (\Sigma Int Int)) : Maybe LBTerm \elim m
  | just (x, y) => natRun op (wrap63 x) (wrap63 y)
  | nothing => nothing

\func rootAt (op : String) (m63 m64 : Maybe (\Sigma Int Int)) : Maybe LBTerm \elim m63
  | just (x, y) => int63Run op x y
  | nothing => int64At op m64

\func int64At (op : String) (m : Maybe (\Sigma Int Int)) : Maybe LBTerm \elim m
  | just (x, y) => int64Run op x y
  | nothing => nothing

-- The instance the agreement checks run against.
--
-- `lbNone` (Semantics/LambdaBoxEval.ard) is the other one: no axiom at all, i.e.
-- λ□ as MetaRocq and CertiRocq have it. The pair is the λ□-side counterpart of
-- `rtLong`/`rtNone`.
\func lbAxioms : LBSpec \cowith
  | axiomArity => axiomArityLb
  | axiomRun => axiomRunLb
